{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he brought near the offering of the people.</b> This is the bringing near of the aspect of the lights planted in Israel from the source of light, which had been altered through the sin so that they would no longer be in the aspect of their original equilibrium to be brought near — for the change in the soul necessitates distancing, for it becomes something of a different kind. And through the service that Aaron performed, Scripture tells that he brought near the offering of the people — meaning, that he brought near all their distancings. Now, logically it would have been fitting for this verse to be written at the end of the offering's procedure, and the reason he began with it is to say that the effect takes place through the intention in the act before it is done, for it is not the deed that is the essence but rather the intention required in it — and this is what Aaron did at the beginning of his actions. Understand this." "And I will hint to you one small detail that Scripture alludes to in its saying <b>and he brought near the olah and performed it according to the law.</b> I have already hinted to you that the remembrance of the olah alludes to Kenesset Yisrael, and through the distancing of Israel from uprightness they bring the olah downward, heaven forfend, and through their drawing near, not only is it not sent away on account of their transgression, but they add to raise it from ascent to ascent until it stands in the order of the law (משפט), as is known to the discerning — and this is ״וַיַּעֲשֶׂה כַּמִּשְׁפָּט״ — he performed it according to the law. I have heard:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:39.761476952Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}